WHAT THIS IS. Issue #41 (SF-2, epic #39) mirrors a shop-floor run into the #40 ledger. be-41-01 shipped scripts/factory/emit.mjs's library half (the never-throwing facade + run sidecar + the seq/lock discipline); this slice adds its CLI half plus the doc clauses. It runs in PARALLEL with be-41-04/05 once be-41-01 has landed — but it edits the same file be-41-01 created, so if both are ever in flight at once they need worktree isolation (conventions.md 2026-08-08). Design of record: .claude/dev-team/tasks/issue-41/architecture-package-v1.md (sections 4 Fork C, 5.2b, 5.5's last two rows, 5.7 C1).

WHY A CLI AT ALL. gates.mjs is a standalone, orchestrator-invoked CLI (#28, merged to main per config.md:55, PR #69 — the citation resolves on this branch). Nothing in a gate run is inside a dispatch.mjs verb, so the only mechanizable way to mirror a gate result is a small CLI the orchestrator invokes with the report path. The same argument does NOT extend to the agent-tool or workflow lanes: they have no code-side hook point at all, so #41 descopes their fidelity (ADR-026 clause 6) and ships at most two best-effort prose lines. team-build.workflow.mjs must stay unaware of cmux and the ledger — pinned by test/cmux-contract.test.mjs:604 and :637.

LEDGER WRITER YOU CALL (scripts/factory/ledger.mjs, NOT edited by #41): recordGateResult({adw_id, phase_id, gate_name, attempt, ok, checks?, violations?, created_at?}) at ledger.mjs:851-880. All five of adw_id/phase_id/gate_name/attempt/ok are REQUIRED (requireFields, which rejects only undefined — so phase_id must be explicit null, never omitted). checks/violations are stored VERBATIM (the writer's own comment, :859-863: 'checks/violations are #28's gates-CLI vocabulary and are stored VERBATIM (structure never re-shaped)'), stringified only at mirror-insert time so replayJsonl round-trips exactly. The row's uniqueness is UNIQUE(adw_id, gate_name, attempt) with INSERT OR IGNORE — a collision is SILENTLY dropped, which is why gate_name must carry the slice and attempt must be a real counter.

CLI SHAPE TO MIRROR — ledger.mjs's own header contract (ledger.mjs:34-41), quoted because it is the pattern:
  // LIBRARY vs CLI: everything below `main()` is a pure library — importing
  // this file performs no I/O, opens no file, installs no signal handler.
  // `main(argv) -> exitCode` never calls process.exit (a piped stdout can be
  // truncated by a synchronous teardown); the `invokedDirectly` guard at the
  // bottom sets `process.exitCode` instead. Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 unexpected
  // internal error, 2 usage / refusal / below floor.
The invokedDirectly guard must realpathSync BOTH sides — dispatch.mjs:2913-2923 is the in-repo shape:
  function realpathOr(path) { try { return realpathSync(path) } catch { return resolvePath(path) } }
  const invokedDirectly = process.argv[1] && realpathOr(process.argv[1]) === realpathOr(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
ledger.mjs's own parseArgs (:1461) and its BOOLEAN_FLAGS set are the argv-parsing shape to copy for `--json`.

REPORT SHAPE (from scripts/chain/gates.mjs's buildReport, :502-508) — see interface_contract for C1's full text. Two traps worth restating here: buildReport has NO warnings key at all (its only warnings-adjacent code folds lintResult.warnings into a COUNT inside a note string, :421-428), so do not look for one; and the {check, detail, severity} object shape that DOES exist lives at dispatch.mjs:1392-1393 and is a different thing entirely (spec-lint warnings). Three unrelated 'warning' shapes, three mappings, never one generic mirror.

WHY stats IS A REQUIRED VERB, NOT A NICETY (section 5.2b): a never-throw facade that silently drops rows would pass 'phases in order, one row per dispatch' on a run that lost half its emissions. So the counters are a NAMED READ SURFACE: `emit.mjs stats [--json]` reads the sidecar and prints {emitted, dropped, lock_giveups, resolution_ambiguous, resolution_missing, payload_keys_dropped}, run-level acceptance requires all drop counters at ZERO, and the live pass must show this verb's output. Note this is distinct from ledger.stats() (ledger.mjs:1164-1166), which reports the HANDLE's own failures (mirror_errors, dropped_payload_keys, degraded_reason) — #41's failures happen BEFORE the handle, in the lock, the resolver and the facade's catch.

DOC SURFACES AND THEIR TEST OWNERS: commands/team.md is pinned by test/commands.test.mjs (and is the ONE commands/*.md already exempt from test/cmux-contract.test.mjs's A9 cmux-vocabulary guard — backend-notes.md 2026-08-04). references/qa-gate.md is pinned by test/qa-gate-doc.test.mjs, whose header carries an 'owned elsewhere' registry naming sections asserted by test/noise-globs.test.mjs instead; that registry must be kept current the moment a section gains a second-purpose test (backend-notes.md 2026-08-08). Both doc test files use the section-scoped extractSection idiom with a distinctive phrase from the new rule — copy it rather than asserting whole-file.

WHY THIS SLICE IS A CODER SPEC AND NOT dev-team:doc-writer (conventions.md 2026-08-08): doc-writer has no Bash and cannot take a spec whose files_in_scope include a test file or whose validation_commands run anything. Two of the three edited surfaces are docs, but the test pins make it a coder spec regardless.

VERSION BUMP: be-41-01's PR-A already bumped .claude-plugin/plugin.json once for its own commit. This slice, as the last one landing in PR-B (be-41-03 -> be-41-04 -> be-41-05, be-41-06 parallel to 04/05), bumps it once more for the whole PR-B commit — read the file's current value at commit time rather than assuming a specific number, since PR-A's exact bump depends on what shipped between planning and execution.

ASSUMPTIONS STATED SO YOU CAN FLAG THEM: (1) one adw_id per (repo, task) run, read from the sidecar be-41-01 created — the CLI never mints one and never reads the mirror to find one. (2) A gate is invoked against a run whose sidecar already exists; if it does not, the verb refuses (a gate result with no run is not something to invent an adw_id for). (3) The mirror covers cmux-mode runs only; an agent-tool run yields zero rows, and the prose must say so plainly rather than implying full coverage — the note for #59 is that retiring hand-written run prose unconditionally would leave a non-cmux run with no record on EITHER surface.
